{"name":"Bear Hug","hex":"#796359","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bear hug","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any especially large, tight or enthusiastic hug, usually friendly and especially given by a male.","A hostile takeover effort in which one firm offers to buy the other firm at a share price too high to refuse."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bear_hug"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bear Hug","blocks":[{"text":"\"Bear Hug\" is the debut single by British musical duo the 2 Bears. It was released on 29 May 2011 as a digital download in the United Kingdom. The song peaked to number 187 on the UK Singles Chart. The song features on the duo's 2011 Extended Play Bearhug and their album Be Strong."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9b/BearHug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_Hug"},{"title":"Bear hug","blocks":[{"text":"The bear hug is a grappling maneuver often seen in sports, such as wrestling. It also exists outside sports, such as street fighting, martial arts personal defense, military hand-to-hand combat, and even affectionate hugging. Bear hugs can be dangerous or even deadly when done with maximum force, and so they are rare in sports, although the simulacrum of a full-force bear hug is common in professional wrestling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Bruno_Sammartino_vs._Billy_Graham_%282%29.jpg/330px-Bruno_Sammartino_vs._Billy_Graham_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_hug"},{"title":"Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Bears are carnivoran mammals of the family Ursidae. They are classified as caniforms within the suborder Caniformia. Although only eight species of bears are extant, they are widespread, appearing in a wide variety of habitats throughout most of the Northern Hemisphere and partially in the Southern Hemisphere. Bears are found on the continents of North America, South America, and Eurasia. Common characteristics of modern bears include large bodies with stocky legs, long snouts, small rounded ears, shaggy hair, plantigrade paws with five nonretractile claws, and short tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg/330px-Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear"},{"title":"Hug","blocks":[{"text":"A hug is a form of endearment, found in virtually all human communities, in which two or more people put their arms around the neck, back, under the armpits or waists of one another and hold each other closely. If more than two people are involved, it may be referred to as a group hug."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Wedding_hugs.jpg/330px-Wedding_hugs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hug"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/500px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/1280px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/500px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUrsidae_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG/500px-Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG/500px-Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plithocyon armagnacensis skull, a member of the extinct subfamily Hemicyoninae from the Miocene","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/500px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/1280px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/500px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Life restoration of Arctotherium bonariense","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArctotherium.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Teufelsh%C3%B6hle-H%C3%B6hlenb%C3%A4r-Dreiviertelprofil.jpg/500px-Teufelsh%C3%B6hle-H%C3%B6hlenb%C3%A4r-Dreiviertelprofil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Teufelsh%C3%B6hle-H%C3%B6hlenb%C3%A4r-Dreiviertelprofil.jpg/1280px-Teufelsh%C3%B6hle-H%C3%B6hlenb%C3%A4r-Dreiviertelprofil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Teufelsh%C3%B6hle-H%C3%B6hlenb%C3%A4r-Dreiviertelprofil.jpg/500px-Teufelsh%C3%B6hle-H%C3%B6hlenb%C3%A4r-Dreiviertelprofil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fossil of the cave bear (Ursus spelaeus), a relative of the brown bear and polar bear from the Pleistocene epoch in Europe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATeufelsh%C3%B6hle-H%C3%B6